From: SYNTRAF
Subject: [PATCH] UDP connect: tolerate out-of-order data packets in --bidir mode too

In --bidir mode the server starts its reverse stream as soon as both streams are
created. When a data packet of that stream reaches the client before the
"accept" reply (reordering: jitter, multipath, Wi-Fi/LTE/satellite links),
iperf_udp_connect() reads it as the reply and fails the whole test with
"unable to read from stream socket". The tolerance for out-of-order packets
already exists for -R (reverse) mode; --bidir has the same reverse stream.

Measured on iperf 3.22.1 with netem "delay 30ms 12ms" on the server side:
5 failed tests out of 12 before, 0 out of 12 after.

Remaining (not addressed): the UDP connect message is sent once and the reply
is never re-requested, so a lost reply blocks the test for 30 s (SO_RCVTIMEO).
---
diff --git a/src/iperf_udp.c b/src/iperf_udp.c
index 023b5f1..68325bb 100644
--- a/src/iperf_udp.c
+++ b/src/iperf_udp.c
@@ -766,7 +766,7 @@ iperf_udp_connect(struct iperf_test *test)
      */
     i = 0;
     max_len_wait_for_reply = sizeof(buf);
-    if (test->reverse) /* In reverse mode allow few packets to have the "accept" response - to handle out of order packets */
+    if (test->reverse || test->bidirectional) /* In reverse and bidirectional modes allow few packets to have the "accept" response - to handle out of order packets */
         max_len_wait_for_reply += MAX_REVERSE_OUT_OF_ORDER_PACKETS * test->settings->blksize;
     do {
         if ((sz = recv(s, &buf, sizeof(buf), 0)) < 0) {
